  "response_complete": true,
  "response_text": "If you have had unprotected sex and are concerned about sexually transmitted infections (STIs), it's important to take steps to protect yourself and your partner. Here are some steps you can take:\n\n1. **Seek Medical Advice**: Contact a healthcare provider or a sexual health clinic to discuss your concerns. They can provide guidance on testing and treatment options.\n\n2. **Consider Testing**: Depending on your symptoms and the type of STI you are concerned about, your healthcare provider may recommend testing. This can include blood tests, urine tests, or swab tests.\n\n3. **Use Condoms**: If you have had unprotected sex, it's important to use condoms consistently and correctly from the start of the sexual encounter to reduce the risk of STIs.\n\n4. **Practice Safe Sex**: Avoid sharing personal items like razors, toothbrushes, or towels that could potentially harbor STI-causing bacteria or viruses.\n\n5. **Inform Partners**: If you have had unprotected sex, inform your sexual partners so they can also get tested and treated if necessary.\n\n6. **Follow Treatment**: If you are diagnosed with an STI, follow the treatment prescribed by your healthcare provider. This will help prevent the infection from spreading and reduce the risk of complications.\n\n7. **Maintain Open Communication**: Discuss your concerns and any symptoms with your partner(s) to ensure they are aware of the situation and can take appropriate action.\n\nRemember, early detection and treatment are crucial in managing STIs effectively. It's important to take these steps seriously to protect your health and the health of your partners.",
